1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
You've classified the error, now you have to act on it, and the recovery almost falls

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,000
straight out of the type.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,000
The common mistake is matching your fix to how the failure feels instead of what it actually is.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:13,000
That's why we classify first.

5
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:21,000
This lesson covers the main recovery paths, and when to fix, when to fall back, and when to escalate.

6
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Let's define three recovery moves up front, because every strategy is a combination of them.

7
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:29,000
First, retry with backoff.

8
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:34,000
That's resending a transient request after a wait that grows on each attempt, so you're

9
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:37,000
not hammering a service that's already busy.

10
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,000
Second, fallback.

11
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:44,000
That's switching to a different model, a simpler path, or a cached result when the primary

12
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:50,000
call can't succeed, so the user still gets an answer instead of an error.

13
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Third, escalation.

14
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:57,000
That's handing the problem to a human, or a support channel, when no automated recovery

15
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:04,000
fits, and you carry the request ID along so whoever picks it up can find the exact call.

16
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Retry, fallback, escalate.

17
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:12,000
The single most important habit here is matching your recovery to the error's type, never to

18
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:14,000
how it looks on the surface.

19
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:19,000
A pair of failures can feel identical and need opposite responses.

20
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:24,000
A slow response and a rate limit both leave you waiting, but one just needs a little patience

21
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,000
while the other needs a real backoff schedule.

22
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Retrying a malformed request feels productive.

23
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:34,000
You're doing something, yet it fixes nothing and burns budget.

24
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:37,000
So resist the urge to react to the symptom.

25
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:42,000
Go back to the classification you already made, transient or permanent, integration

26
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:48,000
or model output, and let that type point you at the response that addresses the cause.

27
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Everything collapses into three recovery buckets.

28
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:54,000
The first is retry and backoff.

29
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,000
That's for transient integration errors, connection drops, a 429, or any 500 series error.

30
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:05,000
You let the wait grow between attempts and honor the retry after header when it's there.

31
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:07,000
The second is fix the request.

32
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:12,000
That's for permanent errors like a 400, a 401, or a 413.

33
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:17,000
You change the payload, the credentials, or the size, then send the corrected call.

34
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:20,000
The third is fix the prompt or fallback.

35
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:23,000
That's for model output problems that ride on a 200.

36
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:27,000
You adjust the prompt, raise a limit, or switch to another model.

37
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:32,000
Three buckets and the error type tells you which one you're in.

38
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Let's zoom in on backoff because it's powerful and it's often misapplied.

39
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Exponential backoff means you wait a little after the first failure, then wait longer

40
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:45,000
after the next, and longer still, giving a busy service room to recover.

41
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:50,000
On a 429, don't guess the interval, honor the retry after header because it tells you

42
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:53,000
exactly how long to hold off.

43
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:58,000
And always cap the number of attempts so a persistent problem fails loudly instead of

44
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:00,000
looping forever.

45
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:01,000
Here's the key limit.

46
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:09,000
Backoff only belongs to transient failures, never to a permanent error like a bad request or a bad key.

47
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:13,000
It helps to know what the SDK already does for you.

48
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:17,000
The official SDKs automatically retry transient failures.

49
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:24,000
And the set is specific, connection errors, 429 rate limits, and 500 series server errors.

50
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:28,000
They use exponential backoff and they honor the retry after header.

51
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:30,000
By default, that's a couple of retries.

52
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:33,000
And you can raise, lower, or disable it.

53
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:38,000
What they won't do is retry a permanent 400 series client error for you.

54
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:45,000
A 400, a 401, a 403, a 413, those come straight back because retrying them is pointless.

55
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:51,000
The 429 is the one 400 series error carved out as transient, which is why it sits in

56
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:53,000
the retried set.

57
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:55,000
And a 409 conflict is its own case.

58
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:59,000
You resolve the conflicting state first, then retry the call.

59
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:04,000
Lean on the SDEC for the transient set and own the rest yourself.

60
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:09,000
When the error is permanent, the whole job is changing something before you try again.

61
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:11,000
On a 400, read the message field.

62
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:15,000
It usually points right at the malformed part, then correct it.

63
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:20,000
On a 401 or 403, the problem is credentials or permissions.

64
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:23,000
So repair the key or grant it access.

65
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:26,000
On a 413, the request is simply too big.

66
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:31,000
So shrink it or split the work across the batch's API when there's a lot of it.

67
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:34,000
And treat a 409 conflict as its own thing.

68
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,000
It means the resource changed under you.

69
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:39,000
So resolve that conflict first, and then retry.

70
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:44,000
In none of these, does the same unchanged call ever start working.

71
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:48,000
Model output problems arrive on a successful 200.

72
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:50,000
So there's no status code to react to.

73
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:53,000
And there are four fixes worth memorizing.

74
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:55,000
First, max tokens.

75
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:58,000
The answer was truncated at the cap you set.

76
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:03,000
So raise max tokens or continue the response from where it stopped.

77
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:06,000
Second, model context window exceeded.

78
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:11,000
The response ran into the model's total context window, not your requested cap.

79
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:18,000
So reduce the input, prune history, or compact the context. Third, refusal.

80
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:20,000
Claude declined on policy grounds.

81
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:26,000
So read stop details for the reason, then adjust the prompt or fall back to another model.

82
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:29,000
Fourth, a clean answer that's simply wrong.

83
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:33,000
That's a prompt and eval problem, and no network retry will fix it.

84
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:37,000
Four different tells, four different fixes.

85
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:43,000
That leaves the two exits when a call can't just be repaired in place, fallback and escalation.

86
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:50,000
A fallback is your automated plan B. You switch to another model, a simpler path, or a cached result.

87
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:55,000
So the user still walks away with something useful instead of an error page.

88
00:05:55,000 --> 00:06:00,000
Escalation is what you reach for when no automated recovery fits at all.

89
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:05,000
You route the failure to a human or a support channel, and you carry the request ID and

90
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:11,000
the error type with it, because that's exactly what lets someone reconstruct what happened.

91
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:14,000
Fallback keeps the system running on its own.

92
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:18,000
Escalation admits a person has to step in.

93
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:19,000
Here's the bottom line.

94
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:24,000
When you choose a recovery, you're really acting on the type you diagnosed.

95
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:30,000
Try and back off for transient issues, fix and request for permanent issues, and adjust

96
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:34,000
the prompt or fallback for model output.

97
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:38,000
The trap is retrying an error that will never clear.

98
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:41,000
That covers selecting the right recovery strategy.

99
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:42,000
I'll catch you in the next one.

